/&gt;<![CDATA[Native Forward Scholars Fund has revealed how it will use the entire $50 million unrestricted donation from philanthropist MacKenzie Scott. The organisation will spend $10 million over the next five years to expand scholarships for Native students, while placing the remaining $40 million into a permanent endowment to provide long-term support. The gift, the largest in the organisation's 57-year history, comes as demand for financial aid continues to rise, with scholars facing an average unmet need of $26,000. Native Forward says the funding will help both current students and future generations pursue higher education.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/education/news/mackenzie-scott-gave-native-forward-50-million-it-will-spend-10-million-on-scholarships-and-place-40-million-in-a-permanent-endowment/articleshow/133031242.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/education/news/mackenzie-scott-gave-native-forward-50-million-it-will-spend-10-million-on-scholarships-and-place-40-million-in-a-permanent-endowment/articleshow/133031242.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-07T17:47:08+05:30</pubDate><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133032444,imgsize-77446.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[New Jersey police officer disguised as a roadside tree catch 74 distracted drivers]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133033500.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[A New Jersey police department used an officer in a ghillie suit resembling a tree. This unusual tactic aimed to raise awareness about distracted driving dangers. Later, plainclothes officers conducted an operation and cited seventy-four drivers. These citations were for using mobile phones while operating vehicles. The campaign sought to reduce preventable crashes caused by inattentive drivers.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/new-jersey-police-officer-disguised-as-a-roadside-tree-helped-catch-74-distracted-drivers-in-just-six-hours/articleshow/133033500.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/new-jersey-police-officer-disguised-as-a-roadside-tree-helped-catch-74-distracted-drivers-in-just-six-hours/articleshow/133033500.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-07T17:44:46+05:30</pubDate><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133033500,imgsize-1489062.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Archaeologists uncover ancient Roman structure near the Colosseum]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133033402.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[In a remarkable discovery, archaeologists have unearthed a second-century building adjacent to the iconic Colosseum in Rome. This newly found structure boasts intricate mosaics and elaborately painted ceilings, hinting at its significant role in ancient society. While it bears resemblance to known firefighters' barracks from that era, some experts speculate it could also have been a luxurious Roman residence. Ongoing research aims to unveil its true historical purpose.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/europe/archaeologists-uncover-ancient-roman-structure-near-the-colosseum-with-mosaics-resembling-firefighters-barracks/articleshow/133033402.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/europe/archaeologists-uncover-ancient-roman-structure-near-the-colosseum-with-mosaics-resembling-firefighters-barracks/articleshow/133033402.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-07T17:33:04+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133033527,imgsize-279560.cms"/></item></channel></rss>